6. Discussion
In this experiment, a lexical analyzer was developed using Flex to recognize and classify tokens of a small C program. The program successfully identified keywords, identifiers, operators, integer constants, and symbols. Each recognized token was displayed with its corresponding token name.
The experiment also showed the importance of rule order and the longest-match principle in Flex. Keywords were placed before the identifier rule, so words like int, if, and return were recognized correctly as keywords. Similarly, == was recognized as a single relational operator, while = was recognized as an assignment operator.
Whitespace and newline characters were ignored using a separate rule. A final catch-all rule was also used to identify unknown characters. This provided a simple way to detect lexical errors.
Through this experiment, the practical concepts of tokens, lexemes, patterns, regular expressions, and pattern matching were understood. Overall, the experiment demonstrated how Flex makes lexical analysis easier by allowing the programmer to define patterns and their corresponding actions.
